invariant: INV-16
file: ct.h
catches: test/widemul-builds.sh
reason: ct.h defines ct_mul128 wherever the compiler has unsigned __int128,
  so a device object built for a 64-bit core can run the 64x64->128
  multiply, and nothing there states its timing: only a host object's
  session does, with CH_CPU_CONSTANT_TIME_MULTIPLY in ch_cfg.cpu. The
  architectures do not promise that timing, which is why Arm publishes
  FEAT_DIT and Intel its DOIT list. Every source still compiles and every
  vector passes. test/widemul-builds.sh requires a unit that calls
  ct_mul128 without -DCH_CPU_RUNTIME not to compile.

--- old
#ifdef CH_CPU_RUNTIME
// The product type of ct_mul128. C11 has no 128-bit integer, and
--- new
#ifdef __SIZEOF_INT128__
// The product type of ct_mul128. C11 has no 128-bit integer, and
